WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.365
Rosa’s disability income application reaches the home office.

00:00:03.365 --> 00:00:06.730
The underwriter must decide whether to accept her,

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:09.675
at what price and with what conditions.

00:00:09.675 --> 00:00:13.881
The criteria are the applicant’s health history, age, occupation, income,

00:00:13.881 --> 00:00:18.087
hobbies, habits such as tobacco use and, for comprehensive medical

00:00:18.087 --> 00:00:22.294
coverage, the limits federal law now places on health-based decisions.

00:00:22.294 --> 00:00:26.921
Disability, long-term care and limited policies may still be fully underwritten.

00:00:26.921 --> 00:00:31.548
The underwriter is matching Rosa to a class of similar risks

00:00:31.548 --> 00:00:35.754
so the price reflects the expected claims for that class.

00:00:35.754 --> 00:00:39.540
Fair classification protects the pool; unfair discrimination is prohibited.

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:41.778
The application is the primary source.

00:00:41.778 --> 00:00:47.001
Part one collects identity, occupation and the coverage requested; part two asks medical questions.

00:00:47.001 --> 00:00:51.850
Rosa signs it, certifying her answers are true to the best of her

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:56.699
knowledge, and it becomes part of the contract when attached to the policy.

00:00:56.699 --> 00:01:00.430
Theo adds a producer’s report, sometimes called an agent’s statement,

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:04.160
giving his own observations: how long he has known her,

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:07.890
whether the coverage replaces another policy, and anything he noticed.

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:13.113
The producer’s report is not shown to the applicant, and it must be honest.

00:01:13.113 --> 00:01:16.470
It is the insurer’s first check on the application.

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:21.496
If Rosa’s answers mention a back injury, the underwriter may request an attending

00:01:21.496 --> 00:01:26.136
physician statement from the doctor who treated her, with her written authorization.

00:01:26.136 --> 00:01:30.775
The APS gives diagnosis, treatment and prognosis in the physician’s own words.

00:01:30.775 --> 00:01:34.255
The underwriter may also order an investigative consumer report,

00:01:34.255 --> 00:01:37.735
often called an inspection report, from an outside agency.

00:01:37.735 --> 00:01:41.988
It covers lifestyle, finances and reputation, drawn from records and interviews.

00:01:41.988 --> 00:01:46.241
The Fair Credit Reporting Act governs that report: Rosa must be

00:01:46.241 --> 00:01:50.494
told it may be ordered, may learn its nature and scope,

00:01:50.494 --> 00:01:54.360
and must be told if it leads to adverse action.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:58.911
The MIB is a membership organization of insurers that keeps coded summaries

00:01:58.911 --> 00:02:03.463
of medical and some nonmedical conditions reported by member companies when a

00:02:03.463 --> 00:02:07.635
person applied for individual life, health, disability or long-term care coverage.

00:02:07.635 --> 00:02:11.048
It exists to detect omissions and fraud across companies.

00:02:11.048 --> 00:02:15.600
The codes flag areas to investigate; an insurer may not decline solely

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:19.772
because of an MIB code and must confirm the underlying facts.

00:02:19.772 --> 00:02:23.564
Applicants receive a notice that the MIB may be consulted,

00:02:23.564 --> 00:02:27.357
and they may request their own file and correct errors.

00:02:27.357 --> 00:02:31.150
MIB codes are not shared with employers or the public.

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:37.047
For larger disability benefits or older applicants the insurer may require a paramedical

00:02:37.047 --> 00:02:42.944
exam, blood and urine tests, or a physician’s examination, at the insurer’s expense.

00:02:42.944 --> 00:02:48.387
Tests screen for conditions such as diabetes, liver disease and nicotine use.

00:02:48.387 --> 00:02:52.924
Testing for HIV requires the applicant’s specific informed consent on

00:02:52.924 --> 00:02:57.460
a separate form that explains the test, how results are

00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:01.542
kept confidential and to whom they may be disclosed.

00:03:01.542 --> 00:03:05.625
Maryland law treats HIV results as confidential medical information.

00:03:05.625 --> 00:03:09.707
An insurer may underwrite on a positive result but

00:03:09.707 --> 00:03:13.790
may not test without consent or reveal results improperly.

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:19.315
The underwriter now has Rosa’s application, Theo’s report, a physician statement about

00:03:19.315 --> 00:03:24.380
her back, an inspection report, an MIB check and lab results.

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:28.063
Each source answers a different question, and each

00:03:28.063 --> 00:03:31.287
carries its own consent or notice requirement.

00:03:31.287 --> 00:03:34.510
The application and medical sources describe health.

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:37.733
The inspection report describes lifestyle and finances.

00:03:37.733 --> 00:03:39.574
The MIB tests consistency.

00:03:39.574 --> 00:03:43.258
None of them is permitted to be used

00:03:43.258 --> 00:03:46.941
for purposes outside underwriting the coverage applied for.

00:03:46.941 --> 00:03:51.546
Lesson 13 shows how the underwriter turns this file into

00:03:51.546 --> 00:03:56.150
a risk class and where the law forbids certain distinctions.
